    "lawCode": "Amd §204, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6671A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nmeetings and absences of the board of regents\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require the Board of Regents to give notice of their\nagenda several days before a scheduled meeting.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Section 1 amends section 204 of the Education Law to\nrequire the Board of Regents to put their official agenda on the\nBoard's official website 7 days in advance. Such notice of time,\nplace, and agenda shall be distributed via the board's official\nInternet website and specifically distributed by United States postal\nto all regents at their usual addresses. An agenda for a regular\nscheduled meeting of the board shall include a list of all items to be\nconsidered with a description of the subject matter of such items to\nbe considered at least three days in advance of such meeting;\nprovided, however, in an instance of a health or safety emergency,\nadditional items may be added to the agenda with less than three days\nnotice. The board shall use best efforts in an instance of a health or\npublic safety emergency to make public the agenda prior to the\nmeeting. An agenda for special meetings of the board of regents shall\nbe made public at least three days prior to such meeting; provided,\nhowever, in an instance of a health or safety emergency, additional\nitems may be added to the agenda with less than three days notice.\n\nA summary of committee reports, any other minutes, and attendance of\nthe board of regents for either regular or special meetings shall be\nelectronically available on the board of regents website at the close\nof each board meeting. The video recording of any regular full board\nmeeting shall be posted no later than five days following the meeting\nof the board. Any meeting summaries and actions taken shall be posted\non the board's website within five days of approval by the board and\nshall remain on the regents' website for a minimum of ten years.  If\nany regents fails to attend three consecutive meetings without a\nsatisfactory excuse, that regent may be deemed to have resigned.\n\nSection 2: Section 2 makes the bill effective the ninetieth day after\nit shall become law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe NYS Board of Regents should hold itself to the highest level of\ntransparency. Allowing the agenda to be given ahead of time would\nallow the stakeholders on several educational issues the appropriate\ntime to respond and discuss the issues. This would encourage more\ninvolvement from the public and would foster an improved dialogue\nbetween both the Board of Regents and other stakeholders in education.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2013: Senate: bill not introduced / Assembly: Passed Assembly\n2012: Senate: Higher Education / Assembly: Education\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome law.\n\n",
